{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Why\nOperator direction (2026-08-15): approach chauvinist-subsolar-arctichare (19ea-29af-ebb3-410b) as an EXPERIMENT. The auto-resume design (adolescent-evilminded-locust) re-runs the whole verifier and relies on the verdict cache to skip credited criteria; it passes NO information about WHERE the failed attempt already searched. Question to answer empirically: does handing a resumption the prior attempt's search frontier as an EXCLUSION list (\"already searched, found nothing: ...\") measurably reduce re-search overlap and steps-to-verdict? Blocker noted at design time: verifier telemetry does not carry argument-level tool-call transcripts — the PoC must capture them harness-side.\n\n## Protocol (rapid PoC — throwaway harness, no production code)\n1. Corpus: >=2 real CLOSED tickets with large surfaces (description near the 8000-char cap, many prose ACs/comments). Run read-only (--source local, never signs). Cache lifecycle (one policy, all steps): each (ticket, arm) pair gets an ISOLATED cache namespace — the harness monkeypatches `cache_dir` (src/rebar/llm/workflow/completion_verdict_cache.py:59, the single derivation of `.rebar/cache/completion_verdicts/...` from `_resolve_root`) to return a per-arm temp dir, the standard unit-suite technique; nothing under the real store is touched. Attempt 1 (forced exhaustion) runs with cache ACTIVE but EMPTY — banking must fill it, and prior real-store verdicts cannot leak in. Its populated cache is then COPIED once and each resume arm reads its own identical copy.\n2. Transcript capture: wrap the verifier's tool executor in-harness (monkeypatch at the workflow seam) to log every tool call with full arguments (file paths read, grep patterns, dirs listed) per criterion.\n3. Force exhaustion: run attempt 1 with a deliberately REDUCED step budget (harness override) so it FAILs insufficiency-only with a partial bank; keep the transcript.\n4. Extract frontier: from the transcript, derive per-criterion searched-set (paths read, patterns tried) and a compact exclusion summary (<=1500 chars).\n5. Resume LOOP per arm, same reduced budget every attempt, each arm on its own copy of the attempt-1 cache (per the step-1 policy): repeat the re-run until overall PASS or 3 resume attempts, whichever first; the cache accrues within the arm across attempts (that accrual IS the resume mechanism). (A) CONTROL — plain re-runs, cache-seeded only (locust behavior). (B) TREATMENT — identical, plus an exclusion summary injected into the verifier prompt, regenerated after each attempt from that arm's cumulative transcript.\n6. Metrics table (the deliverable): re-search overlap rate per attempt (tool calls repeating any earlier attempt's arguments, same arm) A vs B, newly-banked PASSes per step A vs B, attempts-to-clean-close (or 'not clean in 3') A vs B, token cost delta.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] Harness exists in the experiment worktree (throwaway; NOT pushed to Gerrit; no src/rebar changes).\n- [ ] Argument-level transcripts captured for >=2 large tickets across forced-exhaustion + both resume arms.\n- [ ] Metrics table posted as a comment on THIS ticket and on chauvinist-subsolar-arctichare: overlap rate, PASS-per-step, attempts, cost.\n- [ ] Verdict comment: does the exclusion list measurably beat plain cache-seeded resume? Include a go/no-go recommendation and what telemetry production would need to carry.\n- [ ] No store writes beyond ticket comments; no signatures minted (--source local only)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786806138496589001,"uuid":"baab2084-5202-4920-b550-a762ce7f1f1e"}